### Interpreting the combined table

Each row represents one fitted model.

The first set of statistics describes the **overall performance** of that model:

- R²
- Adjusted R²
- RMSE

The second set describes its **incremental improvement** relative to the preceding model:

- ΔR²
- Nested degrees of freedom
- Nested F statistic
- Nested p value

The M3 comparison tests the joint contribution of pay increases after accounting for handling scores of employee complaints and opportunity to learn.

The M0 row has no incremental comparison because it serves as the baseline.

M2 (R² = **0.708**) and M3 (R² = **0.708**) explains the largest proportion of variation in rating measured by R². 

However, M2 (Adjusted R² = **0.686**) has a higher adjusted R² than both M1 (Adjusted R² = **0.67**) and M3 (Adjusted R² = **0.675**). 

Adjusted R² increases slightly when learning is added in M2 but decreases when raises is added in M3. 

In M1, complaints explains (**0.681**) in additional variance compare to M0 measured in ΔR². 

In M2, learning explains (**0.027**) in additional variance compare to M1 measured in ΔR².

In M3, raises explains (**0**) in additional variance compare to M2 measured in ΔR². 

Overall, M1 (complaints) explains the vast majority of variance. Only M1 (p **< .001**) produces statistically significant improvements in fit measured in nested F-tests, whereas adding learning in M2 (p = **0.135**) and raises in M3 (p = **0.876**) does not yield statistically significant improvement. 

Similarly, M1 (RMSE = **6.756**) shows a large, substantively meaningful reduction in RMSE from M0 (RMSE = **11.968**), whereas M2 (RMSE = **6.467**) offers only a modest further reduction and M3 (RMSE = **6.464**) provides none. 

